# ---- raw inputs (policy: docs/DATA-SOURCES.md § "Raw inputs — what is tracked, and why")
#
# The unit of decision is the tree or file a rule covers, because that is the unit
# a command re-creates and the unit someone has to download.
#
# A raw input is COMMITTED when that unit is small AND one of:
#   * a receipt or manifest of what was fetched;
#   * a stable historical record a published number descends from;
#   * a snapshot of a live product the artifacts were derived from — re-fetching
#     a live product returns DIFFERENT data, so the artifacts would stop being
#     derivable from the cache.
# It is IGNORED when that unit is bulk and one committed command re-creates it.
# So `data/raw/usgs/_summary.json` is ignored with the tree it describes although
# it is a 4 KB receipt: it is useless in the repo while the 46 MB it accounts for
# is excluded, and the one command below restores both. The exceptions below are
# file-sized units carved out of a bulk tree.
#
# So every rule below says what re-creates it, and the exceptions that have to
# survive a wholesale exclusion are marked. A rule with no comment above it is a
# half-made decision; pipeline/tests/test_raw_input_policy.py refuses one.
#
# 66 MB / 14 files — HAND v2 for HUC 12070104. Nothing in pipeline/fetch produces
# this: it is a manual archive download, and road_thresholds.py cannot run
# without it. It is the ONE input a clone cannot fetch for itself — the trees
# below it are recoverable with a command, this one is not.
data/raw/hand/
#
# 46 MB / 15 files — daily and annual-peak series for the seven corridor gauges,
# ~7.4 MB per site. Re-created by: node pipeline/fetch/fetch_usgs_history.mjs 1985
data/raw/usgs/*.json
#
# 50 MB / 124 files — 15-minute event caches, one file per (gauge, event), written
# on demand by: python pipeline/analyze/travel_time_subdaily.py
data/raw/usgs/iv/*.json
#
# ...except the ONE interval record the 2017 backcast replays. This one is
# committed: it is the only input to backcast.py, ensemble.py and
# harvey_validation.py and the source of the series the app replays, and it is a
# closed historical event, so it can never be re-fetched to the same value. The
# negation must follow the rule above, and needs no excluded parent directory to
# apply — put the directory form (`data/raw/usgs/`) back and this silently stops
# working, which is how it went undeclared in the first place.
!data/raw/usgs/iv/08114000_20170822.json
#
# 3.4 MB / 14 files — the ingest layer's continuous record, 7 sites x stage+discharge
# for a 7-day window. Re-created by: node pipeline/ingest/refresh.mjs --source=usgs_ogc
data/raw/usgs/ogc/
#
# 1.4 MB / 7 files — Brazos reservoir pool elevation, one per USACE project, from the
# CWMS project inventory + USGS 62614. Re-created by:
#   node pipeline/ingest/refresh.mjs --source=usace
data/raw/usace/
#
# The ingest layer's own receipts are NOT ignored: data/raw/_receipt.json is 40 KB and is
# the only record that these two bulk trees were fetched, from where, and when.
#
# NOT ignored, and deliberately so — no rule below, because there is nothing to exclude:
# data/raw/usfimr/ (4.7 MB / 2 files) holds the SECOND observed extent, the Sentinel-1
# water edge second_event_validation.py refutes thresholds against. It is a fixed release
# file of a closed 2016 event on a university host, so it is committed on the same
# argument as the Harvey extent. Re-created by: node pipeline/fetch/fetch_usfimr_2016.mjs
# ---- end raw inputs

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# vercel deploy token (scripts/deploy-vercel.sh reads it if VERCEL_TOKEN is unset)
.vercel-token
